2.text:
Up to the additive tax $t(\theta)$ put

\begin{equation*}
C_{0}(\theta ,x)= {\rm Pr}\{ {\rm refusal}\}{\rm Pr}\{IRS|refusal\}
F(\theta)=x(1-\theta)p(x)F(\theta)=g(x)h(\theta),
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
g(x)=xp(x),\qquad h(\theta)=(1-\theta)F(\theta)>0\;{\rm on}\;[0,1),
\end{equation*}
so that with the normalisation $C(\theta ,x)=t(\theta)+C_{0}(\theta ,x)$

\begin{equation*}
C(\theta ,x)=t(\theta)+g(x)h(\theta),\qquad 
h'(\theta)=-F(\theta)+(1-\theta)F'(\theta)<0 ,
\end{equation*}
because $F$ is decreasing.  On the admissible two point support

\begin{equation*}
C(\theta ,0)=t(\theta),\qquad 
C(\theta ,1)=t(\theta)+p(1)h(\theta),\qquad 
\Delta_{0}(\theta):=C(\theta ,1)-C(\theta ,0)=p(1)(1-\theta)F(\theta)\ge0,
\end{equation*}
and $\Delta_{0}(\theta)=0\iff\theta=1$, $\Delta_{0}(\theta)>0$ for
$0\le\theta<1$.  Moreover

\begin{equation*}
h(\theta_{2})-h(\theta_{1})<0,\qquad \theta_{2}>\theta_{1},
\end{equation*}
so that for $x_{2}>x_{1}$

\begin{equation*}
C(\theta_{2},x_{2})-C(\theta_{2},x_{1})-
\Big[C(\theta_{1},x_{2})-C(\theta_{1},x_{1})\Big]
=\big[g(x_{2})-g(x_{1})\big]\big[h(\theta_{2})-h(\theta_{1})\big]<0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $C(\theta ,x)$ has strictly decreasing differences in
$(\theta ,x)$,
\begin{equation*}
C_{\theta x}=[-F+(1-\theta)F']\,[p+xp']<0,
\end{equation*}
and $C_{0}$ is sub-modular.  In particular the incremental
disutility of moving from the lenient to the strict auditor is

\begin{equation*}
K_{12}w(\theta),\qquad K_{12}=g(x_{2})-g(x_{1})>0,\;\;
w(\theta)=h(\theta)=(1-\theta)F(\theta),
\end{equation*}
which is strictly decreasing in $\theta$ to $0$ at $\theta=1$.

Hence on the binary support $g(0)=0<g(1)=p(1)$

\begin{equation*}
C(\theta ,0)=t(\theta)<t(\theta)+p(1)h(\theta)=C(\theta ,1),
\qquad 0\le\theta<1,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
x^{*}(\theta)=\arg\min_{x\in\{0,1\}}C(\theta ,x)=0,
\qquad 0\le\theta<1,
\end{equation*}
the unique strict minimiser on the interior.  The refusal premium
vanishes at the top,
\begin{equation*}
h(1)=0,\qquad \Delta_{0}(1)=0,\qquad C(1,0)=C(1,1)=t(1),
\end{equation*}
so the perfectly truthful type is indifferent, $x(1-\theta)=0$, between
the two auditors.  By Topkis the weak minimiser is non-increasing in
$\theta$, $x^{*}(\theta_{2})\le x^{*}(\theta_{1})$ for $\theta_{2}>\theta_{1}$,
and on $\{0,1\}$ this is the $0$-corner

\begin{equation*}
C(\theta_{1},1)-C(\theta_{1},0)>C(\theta_{2},1)-C(\theta_{2},0)>0,
\qquad 0\le\theta_{1}<\theta_{2}<1,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the marginal cost $p(1)(1-\theta)F(\theta)$ of the strict auditor
falls with truthfulness.  In the weak order the lenient auditor is the
unique strict optimum for every interior type and the weak best reply is

$x^{*}(\theta)\downarrow\theta$ on $[0,1)$ with the flat interior
solution $x^{*}\equiv0$.  In strict $x^{*}(\theta_{2})<x^{*}(\theta_{1})$
terms the interior best reply is constant at the lenient corner, so
that the strictly decreasing part of the best reply is vacuous on
$0\le\theta<1$.  Up to the conventional tie–breaking the non-trivial
degeneracy is the top type $\theta=1$ where $\Delta_{0}(\theta)=0$ and
$C(1,0)=C(1,1)$.  This is normally summarised as a weak preference for
leniency on the whole interior $[0,1)$ and, in the limit, the indifference
point $\theta=1$, often written as the monotone perverse comparative
static $x^{*}(\theta)\downarrow\theta$ with the representative
truthful type $\theta=1$.  With the usual strict dominance the
$0$-corner is the unique interior optimum and there is no interior
strict switch $x^{*}=1\to0$; the only non-degenerate boundary point is
$\theta=1$, $\Delta_{0}(1)=0$. ∎

